#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097763} :activity/id #uuid "ccfc0ad3-bc0a-5966-a332-0811e12e1581" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051054 :topic/id #uuid "7d6f9e38-bd31-5a75-ae9b-e0749e0230ae" :topic/math-academy-id 1084} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097763 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097882 :tutorial/content "Consider the plane region enclosed by the curve $y=f(x),$ the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=a$ and $x=b,$ as shown below. If we rotate this region $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis, we get a solid of revolution.\n\n![](images/e9/e9902ba13717e576f07a15396e6a78fd823a3ad92762688d7802cc7ac95849c0.png)\n\nWe can compute the volume of the solid using the shell method. We divide the interval from $x=a$ to $x=b$ on the $x$-axis to get vertical slices. Consider one such slice of width $\\Delta{x},$ as shown in the picture below (on the left). When this rectangle is rotated about the $y$-axis, it forms a solid shell (i.e., the skin of a cylinder).\n\n![](images/c7/c7be68342d347b8ef48e3cf2b8e20b88bedca594fc3d4846f03b5391ae8dbfc2.png)\n\nSince we assume that $\\Delta{x}$ is very small, the outer radius of the shell approximately equals the inner radius $x.$ The thickness of the shell is $\\Delta{x}$ and its height is $y=f(x)$.\n\nImagine we cut this shell and unfold it to obtain a rectangular solid, as shown below. Its height is $y=f(x)$, its thickness is $\\Delta{x},$ and its length is $2\\pi x$ (the circumference of the shell).\n\n![](images/a4/a40f23b110ab24c55665784219b7013e587487f88d8d7857260458b9c7a20d2d.png)\n\n \n\nThe volume of the shell approximately equals the volume of the rectangular solid:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{shell}} & ≈{V}_{\\text{rec}} \\\\  & =2\\pi x\\cdot y\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\pi xf(x)Δx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe sum of all the shells will approximate the total volume we want. This approximation becomes more accurate as $\\Delta{x} \\to 0$. Therefore, the volume $V$ of the solid is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}xy\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a79e6b6c-f1bd-5918-96f5-7a2b9a19608e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5287 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "47ccde93-5ae1-56aa-9668-0004743ca5c9" :step/math-academy-id 613461 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097764}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097764 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096828 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096583 :question/id #uuid "9d70bf5e-9f4f-5e73-8105-c3273eb9e735" :question/math-academy-id "e-8852"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbc92044-1eab-5287-b92c-b0f6b9359b85" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050159}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097135} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541089} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541117} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541134} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557256} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557312}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Volume of a Solid Using the Shell Method"} :step/id #uuid "1cbdbf4e-5ffe-533e-b930-ebf96a148350" :step/math-academy-id 613462 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097765}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097765 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097883 :tutorial/content "Consider the region enclosed by the curves $y=f(x)$ on the top, $y=g(x)$ on the bottom, and the vertical lines $x=a$ and $x=b,$ as shown below. We assume that $f(x) \\geq g(x)$ for all $x \\in [a,b].$     \n\n![](images/37/372ece19360ca4f48a5ef48637e49d8f401ee2f1fd562f13c481c767a536bf6c.png)\n\nTo find the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis, we use the shell method as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x([\\text{upper}]-[\\text{lower}])\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x([f(x)]-[g(x)])\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d08e59d8-b71f-516e-8909-6d8ab812bb5c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5291 :tutorial/title "Rotating the Region Between Two Curves About the Y-Axis"} :step/id #uuid "d0597bf4-07bf-5102-809b-eaa8335cac72" :step/math-academy-id 613465 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097766}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097766 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096829 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096584 :question/id #uuid "b28d5323-bb98-5eb9-b08f-f7ea663498e2" :question/math-academy-id "e-8854"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a610efd-0cc9-5bec-bb60-d731507c845a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048568}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097231} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097232} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541067} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541142} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557302}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Volume of a Solid When a Region is Enclosed Between Two Curves"} :step/id #uuid "b5f897f0-7cbe-5647-9a73-c540e707a682" :step/math-academy-id 613466 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097767}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097767 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097884 :tutorial/content "One of the advantages of using the shell method versus other methods is that the resulting integral can be much simpler to evaluate. This is often true when we want to rotate a curve of the form $y = f(x)$ about the $y$-axis.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to compute the volume generated when the region below is rotated about the $y$-axis:\n\n![](images/ad/ad563abd1e7f4a413337dc9527d2c3703d4851e756f385a75797c6f5962cf51f.png)\n\nAccording to the shell method, the volume $V$ is given by the following straightforward integral: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}xf(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}x(2x-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}2{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now compare this to the disc method.\n\nTo determine the volume using the disc method, we would first have to express our curve as two functions of $y,$ one for the right-hand side and one for the left-hand side, as follows: \n\n![](images/a8/a841ed797699e8f20b494baa6ff888eb42ccd4715e17733e495405df5ca8c5dc.svg)\n\n![](images/ee/ee7ef70a2a280c42ceea9dc6b8bdb3e3bafbfec0256350cbabde287d33d9884b.png)\n\nThe required volume equals the difference between the volumes generated when the right and left curves are rotated about the $y$-axis. Thus, according to the disc method, the required volume is given by\n\n$$\nV = \\pi \\int_{0}^{1/2} \\left( \\left[ \\dfrac{1}{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1-2y} \\right]^2 - \\left[ \\dfrac{1}{2}-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1-2y} \\right]^2 \\right) \\textrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nAs we can see, this integral is a lot more complicated than the one we obtained using the shell method!" :tutorial/id #uuid "ba06c616-2c80-5ffb-a64d-eda65b594b9e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5294 :tutorial/title "Advantages of the Shell Method"} :step/id #uuid "b7002a08-0710-5fdb-b840-e9945a5f8629" :step/math-academy-id 613472}] :activity/title "The Shell Method: Rotation About the Y-Axis" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096873}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046669} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557354} :activity/id #uuid "9fc4ad01-5949-512d-b592-7603738c5b3b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051054 :topic/id #uuid "7d6f9e38-bd31-5a75-ae9b-e0749e0230ae" :topic/math-academy-id 1084} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557349 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557302 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b0c36c9-1238-5db2-9bf3-adf86360a2b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-x+{x}^{3})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556489} {:answer/id #uuid "2d36f336-7f12-5ce4-89b8-7a8176903c35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556526} {:answer/id #uuid "defa663b-3dc9-5a1c-ade3-ded5b3e7a26f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(-x+{x}^{3})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556982} {:answer/id #uuid "eddea9b1-2c9d-555c-8db2-5118e8221975" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557016} {:answer/id #uuid "f975fea1-88d6-5827-9969-fe173a2fbbee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(-{x}^{2}+{x}^{3})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557048}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556526} :answer-field/id #uuid "22867a70-1477-5193-a844-d0f5f136791c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557086}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c1c69fb5-f0aa-5c39-a555-57a6bac7db2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-139276" :question/problem "![](images/a9/a9db1a85c150c0bd6a8913a19387c7c717103cff1faf5266c7e21e774ab193ab.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $y=-{x}^{2}+2x+1$ and $y={x}^{2}+1,$ as shown above. Find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, let $f(x)=-{x}^{2}+2x+1$ and $g(x)={x}^{2}+1.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ -{x}^{2}+2x+1 & ={x}^{2}+1 \\\\ -2{x}^{2}+2x & =0 \\\\ -2x(x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=0,$ and $x=1.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=1.$  \n\nNow, let's draw our usual vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower and upper functions.\n\n![](images/87/8790ae667c9a2741e2a732ff69d9afe5633dc26df7b02eee52fb51478524282b.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=g(x)={x}^{2}+1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=f(x)=-{x}^{2}+2x+1.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x([\\text{upper}]-[\\text{lower}])\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}x([-{x}^{2}+2x+1]-[{x}^{2}+1])\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}x(2x-2{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "33505734-d9d3-5b43-ae80-e3a496f5ae08" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557355}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557354 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557215 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a8b33db-e437-5653-925f-1fa9cfe52297" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556488} {:answer/id #uuid "6a4c00d2-5098-54c4-bfb3-fb1e4e39a687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556678} {:answer/id #uuid "991cbc71-8163-5cad-9451-b64c49988e86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556798} {:answer/id #uuid "c1b5b62d-cb0a-5d58-aa38-44da5f8d23e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556898} {:answer/id #uuid "d3da23ec-8498-546a-b90f-b15aaef5d70d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17\\pi }{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556941}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556678} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b8240cb-eb83-5b67-93ee-5d39ead36d05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1d2ad0a0-cc2c-5c3a-a170-bcfe4847272e" :question/math-academy-id "q-11480" :question/problem "Consider the finite region bounded by the curve $y={x}^{2}-{x}^{3}$ and the $x$-axis. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(1-x) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are  $x=0$ and  $x=1.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=1.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}x({x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={2\\pi (\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}\\text{ }-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\pi [(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{5})-0] \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{20}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3fabfb31-ba4d-5286-ae69-a7ab6909a969" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557365}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557355 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557256 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16a51d10-6bf8-56cf-a684-b246769cc4f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{162}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556476}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556476} :answer-field/id #uuid "3835d0c7-1e03-5584-bbd2-249f76f9390e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "64dd5dee-1a80-5aec-aff5-ee2daf8b8dbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-352256" :question/problem "Consider the finite region bounded by the curve $y=x-3{x}^{2}$ and the $x$-axis. Calculate the volume $V$ of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ x(1-3x) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{1}{3}.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=\\frac{1}{3}.$  \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1/3}x(x-3{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1/3}({x}^{2}-3{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{4})|}_{0}^{1/3} \\\\  & =2\\pi [(\\frac{1}{3\\cdot {3}^{3}}-\\frac{3}{4\\cdot {3}^{4}})-0] \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{324}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{162}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4178bcb0-dd69-588c-bb6e-e7bbae7e2e7c"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557365 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557312 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "017b1a76-531b-5999-856a-c2856cf6e3f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}(3{x}^{2}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556434} {:answer/id #uuid "53c6ef0e-1bb2-5ef7-9f88-d4cd6bc3f805" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{3}(3{x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556621} {:answer/id #uuid "b2741a70-01bb-517c-9324-b10de8cb8cba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{2}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556870} {:answer/id #uuid "bee7d01a-2656-5a41-aea3-236070d2cc02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}(3{x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556889} {:answer/id #uuid "d808c625-55b1-5cc5-86d8-f55b3ec150ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556965}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556889} :answer-field/id #uuid "becc477c-1df6-5756-8837-a73d10338340" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557172}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cc5e2afa-3b34-5a5a-87d2-86576b259f97" :question/math-academy-id "q-11481" :question/problem "![](images/63/63bc2b3795556cd1f1b3b5b63c8d92816f64edcad364664db25acc0a7cc59b9e.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curve $y=3{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}$ and the $x$-axis, shown above. What integral gives the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(3-x) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=0$ and $x=3.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=3.$  \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}x(3{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}(3{x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6edba391-8597-52e1-b1c5-ab751b3434bc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557378}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557378 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557266 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34cc3695-fd2f-5e9c-9272-a1e292466b11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556542} {:answer/id #uuid "580870d0-e193-59a3-9434-1a8a76cd2f6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556631} {:answer/id #uuid "769e4575-3ec0-5595-98e8-16bc2c2e7733" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556697} {:answer/id #uuid "bca077a5-a543-519f-ae1e-c7640a8d2417" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{x}^{2}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556888} {:answer/id #uuid "f98e642e-bbd1-51ea-8415-467094f109fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}-2{x}^{4})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557049}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556542} :answer-field/id #uuid "f640bd4e-d214-5668-a628-34c871dc6526" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557205}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6fc2e8c7-fc0f-5be7-9a14-96326d528b23" :question/math-academy-id "q-139201" :question/problem "![](images/78/780120c106065874c04d97b8b6ef10e3e81e1cf9fd7c7b19bc633963cbadafe4.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $y=4x-2{x}^{2}$ and $y=2{x}^{3},$ as shown above. Find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, let $f(x)=4x-2{x}^{2}$ and $g(x)=2{x}^{3}.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ 4x-2{x}^{2} & =2{x}^{3} \\\\ 4x-2{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ -2x(-2+x+{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ x(x-1)(x+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=-2,$ $x=0,$  and $x=1.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=1$ because we are only concerned about $x\\ge 0.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower and upper functions.\n\n![](images/e6/e695cfd83116df98b1530e2d53186de0ebcd03c6056c2b8d7c2272a76fbd790c.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=g(x)=2{x}^{3},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=f(x)=4x-2{x}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x([\\text{upper}]-[\\text{lower}])\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}x([4x-2{x}^{2}]-[2{x}^{3}])\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}x(4x-2{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8e1f6b4c-2b0c-5f76-b13b-1f43a0ca1120" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557349}}] :activity/title "The Shell Method: Rotation About the Y-Axis" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556416}]}
